  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is as follows:\n\n*$S$ is **not** a vector space (with respect to polynomials addition and multiplication of a polynomial by a real number) because **multiplication of functions in  $S$ by real scalars is not closed.** *\n\nIndeed, let's consider, for example, the polynomial  $f(x)=x.$ Then $f\\in S$, but $\\sqrt{2}f(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{\u00a0}x\\notin S$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\nOn the other hand: \n\n- Addition of polynomials is associative. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- Addition of polynomials is commutative. $\\,\u2713$\n\n- The zero function belongs to $S.$ $\\,\u2713$\n\n- $1\\cdot f=f$ for all $f\\in S.$ $\\,\u2713$",
